.Dd June 25, 2026
.Dt SLCAND 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm slcand
.Nd serial-line CAN bridge daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl cov
.Op Fl b Ar baud
.Op Fl s Ar code
.Op Fl y Ar code
.Op Fl w Ar seconds
.Ar device
.Op Ar ifname
.Nm
.Op Fl cov
.Op Fl s Ar code
.Op Fl y Ar code
.Op Fl w Ar seconds
.Fl p Ar port
.Op Ar ifname
.Nm
.Op Fl cov
.Op Fl s Ar code
.Op Fl y Ar code
.Op Fl w Ar seconds
.Fl t Ar host : Ns Ar port
.Op Ar ifname
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon bridges a serial-line CAN
.Pq Dq slcan
adapter, or a TCP connection, to a
.Xr slcan 4
CAN network interface.
.Fx
has no in-kernel slcan line discipline, so the slcan ASCII protocol is handled
in userspace by
.Nm
instead.
.Pp
The daemon creates the named CAN interface
.Pq Ar ifname , default Dv slcan0 ,
brings it up, opens its backing character device, and then forwards frames in
both directions: frames an application sends on the interface are encoded as
slcan ASCII and written to the
.Ar device ,
and slcan frames read from the
.Ar device
are injected as received frames.
.Pp
With
.Fl p
or
.Fl t
the slcan ASCII stream is carried over TCP instead of a serial port, which lets
two machines share one virtual CAN bus.
The channel-setup options
.Fl o , Fl c , Fl s
and
.Fl y
apply to the TCP link as well, so
.Nm
can also drive a real network-attached slcan adapter that listens on a TCP port:
.Fl o
sends the open command needed to put such an adapter on-bus.
A peer
.Nm
ignores these commands, so they are harmless when tunnelling
between two daemons.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b Ar baud
Set the serial baud rate
.Pq default 115200 .
.It Fl c
Send a close-channel command to the adapter on exit.
.It Fl o
Send an open-channel command to the adapter at startup.
.It Fl p Ar port
Act as a TCP server, listening on
.Ar port .
.It Fl s Ar code
Set the nominal CAN bitrate using the adapter bitrate code 0 to 8
.Pq Dv S0 No = 10 kbit/s ... Dv S6 No = 500 kbit/s ... Dv S8 No = 1 Mbit/s .
.It Fl t Ar host : Ns Ar port
Act as a TCP client, connecting to
.Ar host
on
.Ar port .
If the connection cannot be established or is later lost,
.Nm
retries every few seconds, keeping the CAN interface up across the gap.
.It Fl v
Be verbose: print each bridged frame to standard output, tagged with the
interface name and a direction of
.Dq RX
(a frame received from the bus) or
.Dq TX
(a frame sent to the bus).
.It Fl w Ar seconds
Wait up to
.Ar seconds
for the serial
.Ar device
to appear before giving up.
This is useful at boot, when a USB adapter may still be enumerating.
.It Fl y Ar code
Set the CAN FD data-phase bitrate code, enabling CAN FD on the link.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Pa
.It Pa /dev/slcan Ns Ar N
character device backing the
.Xr slcan 4
interface
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Bring up
.Dv slcan0
on a USB adapter at 500 kbit/s:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slcand -o -s6 /dev/cuaU0 slcan0
candump slcan0
.Ed
.Pp
Attach a network-attached slcan adapter that listens on TCP port 2000, at
500 kbit/s:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slcand -o -s6 -t 192.0.2.10:2000 slcan0
candump slcan0
.Ed
.Pp
Tunnel a CAN bus between two hosts over TCP:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hostA# slcand -p 29536 slcan0
hostB# slcand -t hostA:29536 slcan0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr can 4 ,
.Xr slcan 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Vladimir Goncharov Aq Mt devel@viruzzz.org
.Sh CAUTION
The slcan ASCII protocol carries only classic CAN frames unless CAN FD is
enabled with
.Fl y ;
when bridged over TCP the data is unauthenticated, so use it only on trusted
networks.
